---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import CommitValidator from '../../components/CommitValidator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Semantic Commit Validator" description="Check a commit message against Conventional Commits format rules.">
  <CommitValidator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why validate before committing</h2>
  <p>Many teams enforce Conventional Commits with a git hook (like commitlint) that rejects malformed messages at commit time — checking your message format here first avoids a rejected commit and having to rewrite it.</p>

  <h2>How to use this validator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste your draft commit message.</li>
    <li>Review any flagged formatting issues.</li>
    <li>Fix and re-check before running <code>git commit</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p><code>feat(auth): add password reset flow</code> passes validation — a valid type, an optional scope in parentheses, a colon, and a lowercase description.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Capitalizing the description</strong> or ending it with a period, both of which violate the common convention (though not the core spec itself).</li>
    <li><strong>Using an invalid type</strong> not in the standard set (feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, build, ci, chore, revert).</li>
    <li><strong>Missing the colon-space separator</strong> between the type/scope and the description.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Double-checking a commit message before pushing to a repo with enforced commit linting</li>
    <li>Learning Conventional Commits format through examples</li>
    <li>Reviewing a batch of commit messages for consistency</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this replace a commit hook like commitlint?</strong> No — this is a manual check; for automatic enforcement, set up commitlint or a similar tool as a git hook in your repository.</p>
</ToolLayout>
